<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<robot name="washroom_robot">
  <!--
  washroom_robot.urdf : ONE self-contained file. No meshes, no xacro: every shape is a box or cylinder
  measured from the STEP. The CAD is almost entirely boxes and cylinders; only the wheel-hub profile and
  the bumper edge fillets are simplified.

  SOURCES
    Geometry : wholerobotstepfile001.step  (Shapr3D 26.180.0.11926, exported 2026-10-26T20:46:53+05:30, units mm)
    Meaning  : Washroom_Robot_Prototype_Sensors_and_BOM.pdf  (sensor placement + BOM, prices as of 7 Oct 2026)
    Generator: build_urdf.py (re-run after CAD changes; it refuses to run if a body changes size/count)

  FRAMES  (REP-103: x forward, y left, z up; metres, radians)
    CAD to ROS: x = -Y_cad, y = +X_cad, z = Z_cad + 148.9 mm (lowest wheel points define the floor).
    Forward = -Y_cad agrees with three separate cues: the L2 sits behind centre (PDF x -0.12), the D435i and
    2D lidar sit on the -Y face, and the tall box (rear in the PDF) is at +Y.
    base_link      ROOT, above the CAD origin (= wheel-rectangle centre within 0.3 mm) at axle height 0.1025 m.
                   Odometry should therefore publish odom -> base_link.
    base_footprint fixed child of base_link on the floor = the PDF schedule frame ('robot centre on the floor').

  TREE (all joints fixed except the wheels)
    base_link
      +- base_footprint
      +- chassis_link                       body envelope, carries the body mass
      +- {front,rear}_{left,right}_wheel_link   continuous, axis +y, positive = rolls forward
      +- unitree_l2_link
      +- d435i_link
      |    +- d435i_optical_frame           z forward, x right, y down
      +- lidar_2d_link
      +- ultrasonic_{front,rear}_{left,right}_link    +x along the beam
      +- cliff_tof_{front,rear}_{left,right}_link     +x straight down
      +- bumper_{front,rear}_link                     +x outward

  SENSOR FRAMES vs PDF SCHEDULE  (base_footprint frame, m)
    frame                        this URDF                PDF                  source of the URDF value
    unitree_l2_link              (-0.100, 0.000, 0.799)   (-0.12, 0.00, 0.78)  x,y = STEP body; z = STEP box top 0.737 (PDF assumed 0.72) + 30 mm standoff + half of 65 mm
    d435i_link                   (0.404, 0.000, 0.264)    (0.35, 0.00, 0.26)   front (glass) face of STEP body; STEP front face is at x 0.399, PDF assumed 0.35
    lidar_2d_link                (0.400, 0.000, 0.130)    (0.37, 0.00, 0.13)   x,y = STEP body centre (protrudes 21 mm past the face); z = PDF scan plane
    ultrasonic_front_left_link   (0.408, 0.191, 0.296)    (0.35, 0.17, 0.30)   STEP body, centre of outward face
    ultrasonic_front_right_link  (0.408, -0.191, 0.296)   (0.35, -0.17, 0.30)  STEP body, centre of outward face
    ultrasonic_rear_left_link    (-0.401, 0.211, 0.298)   (-0.35, 0.17, 0.30)  STEP body, centre of outward face
    ultrasonic_rear_right_link   (-0.401, -0.211, 0.298)  (-0.35, -0.17, 0.30) STEP body, centre of outward face
    cliff_tof_front_left_link    (0.354, 0.208, 0.119)    (0.33, 0.21, 0.08)   STEP body, centre of downward face
    cliff_tof_front_right_link   (0.354, -0.208, 0.119)   (0.33, -0.21, 0.08)  STEP body, centre of downward face
    cliff_tof_rear_left_link     (-0.354, 0.208, 0.119)   (-0.33, 0.21, 0.08)  STEP body, centre of downward face
    cliff_tof_rear_right_link    (-0.354, -0.208, 0.119)  (-0.33, -0.21, 0.08) STEP body, centre of downward face
    Why x differs: the PDF numbers assume a 0.70 x 0.50 x 0.72 m body; the STEP body is
    0.79 x 0.51 x 0.737 m, so front/rear sensors sit ~5 cm further out.

  ASSUMPTIONS  (no masses or densities were supplied; edit the <mass> tags, inertias scale linearly)
    1. chassis_link 25.0 kg = frame, panels, battery, 4 drive motors, electronics AND all
       sensors. Wheels 1.5 kg each. Total 31.0 kg.
    2. Chassis inertia: that mass spread evenly over the outer envelope, COM (-0.065, 0.000, 0.345) m
       above the floor. Rough: the real COM is probably lower (battery, motors). The CAD cover itself was not
       used for mass because it is an irregular shell (1 mm right wall, 111 mm solid left wall, 41 mm top).
    3. Wheel inertia: the STEP wheel solid scaled to 1.5 kg.
    4. Sensor and bumper links are massless; no sensor specs (FOV, range, rate, optical centres) are encoded,
       because the PDF does not state them. (MuJoCo infers ~2 kg for them from geometry unless
       compiler inertiafromgeom is false; Gazebo lumps them as massless.)
    5. L2 height follows the PDF 30 mm standoff rule, not the STEP (STEP has it sunk 40 mm into the box top).
    6. D435i pitch (8 deg nose-down) is from the PDF; the STEP body is not pitched.
    7. 2D-lidar scan height (0.13 m) is from the PDF; the STEP placeholder box is drawn on its side.

  NOT MODELLED
    - E-stop: the PDF puts it at the rear-face centre at 0.60 m; in this STEP that spot is inside the open rear notch.
    - A 90x25x25 mm block (D435i-sized) buried inside the right-rear top corner at (-0.32, -0.20, 0.71): not in the PDF.
    - Arm (not in the STEP), internal electronics (no frames needed), mecanum rollers.

  CHECK ON THE ROBOT: total mass; L2 height and which way its x-axis points; D435i pitch; 2D-lidar scan height
  and 0-degree direction; cliff ToF height above floor; front bumper vs lidar protrusion; loaded wheel radius.

  MECANUM CONTROLLER NUMBERS: wheel radius 0.1025 m, half wheelbase lx 0.2360 m, half track ly 0.2150 m.

  VIEW / CHECK
    check_urdf washroom_robot.urdf
    urdf_to_graphviz washroom_robot.urdf
    ros2 launch urdf_tutorial display.launch.py model:=$PWD/washroom_robot.urdf
-->
  <!-- ===== Materials (sensor colours match the PDF callouts) ===== -->
  <material name="cad_grey">
    <color rgba="0.352 0.352 0.371 1"/>
  </material>
  <material name="tyre_black">
    <color rgba="0.1 0.1 0.1 1"/>
  </material>
  <material name="hub_grey">
    <color rgba="0.55 0.55 0.58 1"/>
  </material>
  <material name="marker_white">
    <color rgba="0.92 0.92 0.92 1"/>
  </material>
  <material name="l2_purple">
    <color rgba="0.48 0.45 0.85 1"/>
  </material>
  <material name="mount_white">
    <color rgba="0.85 0.85 0.85 1"/>
  </material>
  <material name="d435i_green">
    <color rgba="0.12 0.62 0.47 1"/>
  </material>
  <material name="lidar_orange">
    <color rgba="0.85 0.37 0.19 1"/>
  </material>
  <material name="ultrasonic_ochre">
    <color rgba="0.72 0.46 0.1 1"/>
  </material>
  <material name="tof_blue">
    <color rgba="0.23 0.53 0.84 1"/>
  </material>
  <material name="bumper_red">
    <color rgba="0.88 0.28 0.28 1"/>
  </material>
  <!-- ===== base_link: ROOT. Directly above the CAD origin / PDF 'robot centre', at wheel-axle height (0.1025 m). Frame only - no inertia here, so KDL/robot_state_publisher does not warn about an inertial root; the body mass lives in chassis_link (Gazebo lumps the fixed joint). ===== -->
  <link name="base_link"/>
  <!-- base_footprint: floor projection of base_link = the PDF's 'origin at robot centre on the floor' frame. -->
  <link name="base_footprint"/>
  <joint name="base_footprint_joint" type="fixed">
    <parent link="base_link"/>
    <child link="base_footprint"/>
    <origin xyz="0 0 -0.1025" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  </joint>
  <!-- ===== chassis_link: outer envelope of STEP body 'Cover' rebuilt from its planar faces as 18 boxes (rotated ones follow the 60 deg front slope and the 20 deg rear-notch floor and its pad; the underside slab is split to leave an open pocket under each cliff sensor, as the STEP body is open underneath). Same boxes are used for collision. Inertial = assumed mass spread uniformly over that envelope (ROUGH: real COM is probably lower). ===== -->
  <link name="chassis_link">
    <inertial>
      <origin xyz="-0.065 0 0.2426" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <mass value="25.000"/>
      <inertia ixx="1.25341" ixy="0" ixz="0.331293" iyy="1.83996" iyz="0" izz="1.69493"/>
    </inertial>
    <!-- underside slab 1/9 (leaves the cliff-sensor pockets open) -->
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="-0.3805 0 0.0224" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <box size="0.021 0.51 0.0521"/>
      </geometry>
      <material name="cad_grey"/>
    </visual>
    <!-- underside slab 2/9 (leaves the cliff-sensor pockets open) -->
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="-0.3535 -0.2449 0.0224" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <box size="0.033 0.0202 0.0521"/>
      </geometry>
      <material name="cad_grey"/>
    </visual>
    <!-- underside slab 3/9 (leaves the cliff-sensor pockets open) -->
